6051003 #edb/ref 1034 1.3070450000428422 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051003 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "78b2a9f9-2416-5777-955d-8367f028782b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115139 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115139 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $9x^4 = \\dfrac{1}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "977cdc31-054e-5910-a649-e604dc4ce31f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115139 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $x^4$ term and then taking the $4$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $x^4$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $4$th root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since the exponent $(4)$ is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{81} \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{\\frac{1}{81}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\dfrac13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115140 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115140 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $(x-3)^4=-\\dfrac{1}{81}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f41b040-6584-57cd-8d47-b01e071b0fa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115140 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $(x-3)^4 \\geq 0$ for all values of $x$ because the exponent ($4$) is even. This expression can never be negative. \n\nTherefore, there are no real solutions to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Equations With Even Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115310 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b936abcf-8e73-524c-a9bf-e59b55a48f5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Equations Containing an Expression Raised to an Even Power" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115311 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "23e661ed-3a7b-598a-a7b1-fd5011fe4fb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Equations With Even Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115421 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b0012b09-9794-5364-a20b-bf1c5e744a22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1061 30068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cf6da35-b32f-503b-b8ea-94efb7441f1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116226 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116227 #edb/ref 1061 30120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116227 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c66f2f53-2687-55ab-8bb5-e1056e5074bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116227 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1061 30128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "036c0d84-9bf6-5da8-986c-2ace277257a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an even exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^6 = 64?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince the exponent $(6)$ is even, there will be two real solutions (positive and negative):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{{x}^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is even, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$ with $a \\geq 0,$ we get two solutions (positive and negative):* \n\n$$\nx = \\pm\\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If $a<0$ and $n$ is even, equation  $x^n = a$ has no solution since, in this case, $x^n \\geq 0$ for any real $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1065 4833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6a9834f3-f04b-5340-b297-793072b594d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116534 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494327 #edb/ref 1080 "$1$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494327 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494327 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "046961d9-841f-51f7-ba46-6248fd584f28" #edb/ref 1759218