7592186045417] [17592186495082 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $8$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ Since the exponent $(8)$  is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{8} & =256 \\\\ \\sqrt[8]{{x}^{8}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[8]{256} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[8]{{2}^{8}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495083 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation ${x}^{6}=64.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49a4d1a9-08de-560d-b9e9-2048bdc86e7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495083 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ Since the exponent $(6)$  is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{{x}^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495103 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $8(x+3)^{6}=512.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80133e3d-6a6e-531f-96e9-2c88946d7720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495103 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+3)^{6}$ term and then taking the $6$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+3)^{6}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(x+3)^{6} & =512 \\\\ (x+3)^{6} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $6$th root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since the exponent $(6)$ is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{(x+3)^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495166 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495166 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495166 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495166 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $\\frac{(x+4)^{4}}{25}=25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495166 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f481106a-9bb9-56a6-bdda-a603ba8e3a03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495166 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+4)^{4}$ term and then taking the $4$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+4)^{4}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{4}}{25} & =25 \\\\ (x+4)^{4} & =625\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $4$th root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since the exponent $(4)$ is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{4} & =625 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x+4)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{625} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{5}^{4}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-9$ and $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
